# 1. Verification Usage Conditions

## 1.1. Public Source Code — Free Verification

Verification of Public Source Code is permitted without payment of any license fee under this License.

This permission applies to both personal and commercial use, subject to compliance with all other provisions of this License.

A Licensee may use Axiom to verify Public Source Code for commercial development, production, research, education, or any other lawful purpose without purchasing a Commercial Agreement.

However, the permission to perform free Verification of Public Source Code does not include the right to provide a Competing Service, as defined in Section 2.

## 1.2. Private Source Code — Commercial Agreement Required

Verification of Private Source Code requires a valid Commercial Agreement.

Unless the exception in Section 1.3 applies, a Licensee must not use the Covered Work to perform Verification of Private Source Code for commercial, organizational, production, or business purposes without an applicable Commercial Agreement.

This requirement applies regardless of whether Verification is performed locally, on-premises, in a private cloud, or through a hosted environment.

The applicable Commercial Agreement shall separately establish the permitted scope, terms, and fees for such Verification.

For the avoidance of doubt, "commercial, organizational, production, or business purposes" includes Verification performed by or for a company, organization, or other legal entity in connection with its operations, including internal development, testing, and production, regardless of whether the results are shared outside that organization.

## 1.3. Exception — Non-Commercial Personal Use

An individual may use the Covered Work to verify Private Source Code without a Commercial Agreement if the use qualifies as Non-Commercial Personal Use.

This exception is limited to the individual's own personal hobby, learning, research, and experimentation.

This exception does not include:

1. Verification performed for an employer or client.
2. Verification performed as part of paid work.
3. Verification performed on behalf of a company or organization.
4. Provision of Verification as a paid or otherwise commercially compensated service.
5. Use by or on behalf of a legal entity or organization.

## 1.4. Mixed Verification

If a single Verification execution includes both Public Source Code and Private Source Code within the same Verification Scope, that execution shall be treated as Verification of Private Source Code.

Accordingly, a Commercial Agreement is required unless the execution qualifies for the exception in Section 1.3.

A Licensee may perform separate Verification executions limited exclusively to Public Source Code without payment of a license fee.

## 1.5. Verification Scope and Classification

The classification of a Verification Target shall be determined at the time the relevant Verification execution begins, based on the actual source code included in its Verification Scope.

The Verification Scope includes the source code and related dependencies that Axiom actually reads, loads, analyzes, or includes in its verification or proof obligations.

The fact that some files, dependencies, or related repositories are publicly accessible does not, by itself, qualify the entire Verification Target as Public Source Code.

The classification of source code as Public Source Code or Private Source Code shall be determined in accordance with Section 3.

The following classification rules apply:

1. The Verification Scope is determined by the source code actually included in the analysis, verification, or proof obligations of the execution, not by the entirety of the containing repository or project.
2. Each item of source code in the Verification Scope is classified as Public Source Code or Private Source Code as of the time the Verification execution begins, in accordance with Section 3.5.
3. If every item of source code in the Verification Scope is Public Source Code, the execution is Verification of Public Source Code.
4. If any item of source code in the Verification Scope is Private Source Code, the execution is Verification of Private Source Code.
5. Mere dependency on, or inclusion of, a publicly available library or public dependency in the Verification Scope does not, by itself, make the execution Verification of Private Source Code; classification follows the actual public or private status of each item of source code in the Verification Scope.
6. A Verification execution that includes any Private Source Code in its Verification Scope is treated as Verification of Private Source Code in its entirety, and the public portions of that execution may not be split off to characterize the execution as free; separate executions limited exclusively to Public Source Code are permitted without payment of a license fee.
7. The licensee's choice to keep Verification results, reports, or evidence private does not restrict, condition, or qualify the permission to perform free Verification of Public Source Code under Section 1.1.

## 1.6. Historical Public Verification

If source code qualified as Public Source Code when a Verification execution was performed, the permission granted for that completed execution shall remain valid even if the source code is subsequently made Private Source Code.

A Licensee may continue to retain, reproduce, and use the resulting Verification reports, records, and evidence, subject to this License and applicable third-party rights.

However, this Section does not authorize new Verification executions after the source code becomes Private Source Code without an applicable Commercial Agreement or the exception in Section 1.3.

## 1.7. Verification Results and Evidence

A Licensee may retain Verification results, reports, logs, certificates, and other evidence generated through authorized use of the Covered Work.

A Licensee is not required to publish such results or evidence merely because the Verification Target consists of Public Source Code.

The public availability of a Verification Target does not impose any obligation to disclose the Licensee's Verification results, internal analysis, reports, or derived evidence.

## 1.8. No Obligation to Disclose Verification Targets

Nothing in this License requires a Licensee to disclose the source code, repositories, Verification Targets, Verification results, or evidence associated with its use of Axiom.

The classification of a Verification Target determines the applicable Verification usage conditions only. It does not create an obligation to publicly disclose any source code or Verification materials.

